// Reference snippet: named glyph constants.
//
// NOTE: Pine Script has no local file imports — paste these, don't import them.
//
// Why constants: glyphs scattered as literals drift (one file uses ✔, another ✓),
// and they render at different widths across platforms, which breaks column
// alignment in tables and tooltips. Naming them makes a swap one edit, and keeps
// the set small enough to stay visually consistent.
//
// Width warning: ★ ▲ ● are full-width in most fonts; ✔ ✘ ↑ ↓ are not. Don't mix
// widths inside a column you want aligned — pick one family per column.
//
// This is a fragment, not a standalone script:
// PINE051 too: every constant here is declared FOR the reader to copy,
// so nothing in this file reads them.
// pine-lint-disable PINE001,PINE002,PINE027,PINE051

// ————— Strength (full-width, safe to repeat in a row)
string GLYPH_STAR_ON  = "★"
string GLYPH_STAR_OFF = "☆"

// ————— Direction (full-width)
string GLYPH_UP   = "▲"
string GLYPH_DOWN = "▼"
string GLYPH_FLAT = "▬"

// ————— Direction (narrow — for inline text, not columns)
string GLYPH_ARROW_UP   = "↑"
string GLYPH_ARROW_DOWN = "↓"

// ————— Verdicts (narrow — tooltips and single cells)
string GLYPH_PASS = "✔"
string GLYPH_FAIL = "✘"
string GLYPH_NONE = "–"

// @function Repeats an on/off glyph pair into a filled/empty meter.
// @param score How many positions are filled.
// @param maxScore Total positions.
glyphMeter(int score, int maxScore, string onGlyph, string offGlyph) =>
    int filled = math.max(0, math.min(maxScore, score))
    str.repeat(onGlyph, filled) + str.repeat(offGlyph, math.max(0, maxScore - filled))
